{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","statement":"The paper presents a novel reordering approach for machine translation utilizing sparse features based on dependency word pairs.","source_span":"we present a novel reordering approach utilizing sparse features based on dependency word pairs"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","statement":"Experiments on Chinese-to-English translation show a statistically significant improvement of 1.21 BLEU points using the proposed approach compared to a state-of-the-art SMT system with prior reordering approaches.","source_span":"Experiments on Chinese-to-English translation show a statistically significant improvement of 1.21 BLEU point using our approach, compared to a state-of-the-art statistical MT system that incorporates prior reordering approaches."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","statement":"Each dependency word pair feature captures whether two words related by a dependency link in the source sentence follow the same order or are swapped in the translation output.","source_span":"Each instance of these features captures whether two words, which are related by a dependency link in the source sentence dependency parse tree, follow the same order or are swapped in the translation output."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","statement":"The sparse feature functions are based on the pre-ordering rules of BIBREF6, but unlike that manual rule-based approach, the weights are automatically learned and used during translation without an explicit pre-ordering step.","source_span":"in contrast to the manual rule-based pre-ordering approach of BIBREF6, the weights of our sparse feature functions are automatically learned and used during the actual translation process, without an explicit pre-ordering step"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","statement":"Feature functions are defined for each word pair by dependency labels, POS tags, and the combination of both, resulting in four feature functions for every word pair ordering.","source_span":"we define our feature functions for each word pair by the dependency labels, the POS tags, and the combination of both, resulting in a group of four feature functions for every word pair ordering"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","statement":"Two types of feature function templates are defined: head-child and sibling.","source_span":"we define two types of feature function templates, namely head-child and sibling"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","statement":"The dependency swap rule template defines rule instances based on word pairs with a head-child or sibling relationship.","source_span":"which defines rule instances based on the word pairs with head-child or sibling relationship"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How much did the proposed dependency word pair reordering approach improve BLEU score on Chinese-to-English translation?","answer":"It achieved a statistically significant improvement of 1.21 BLEU points over a state-of-the-art SMT system that incorporates prior reordering approaches."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What do the dependency word pair sparse features in this MT reordering approach actually capture?","answer":"They capture whether two words linked by a dependency relation in the source sentence's parse tree follow the same order or are swapped in the translation output."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How many feature functions does the approach define for each word pair ordering, and what are they based on?","answer":"Four feature functions per word pair ordering, based on dependency labels, POS tags, and the combination of both."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"What are the two types of feature function templates defined, and what relationships do they cover?","answer":"Head-child and sibling templates, covering word pairs that have a head-child or sibling relationship in the dependency parse tree."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How does this paper's approach differ from BIBREF6's manual rule-based pre-ordering approach, even though it builds on the same rules?","answer":"Instead of manually specifying which dependency labels trigger word swaps, the paper's feature weights are learned automatically and applied during actual translation, with no separate pre-ordering step."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0064","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What kind of features does the paper's novel MT reordering approach use?","answer":"It uses sparse features based on dependency word pairs from the source sentence's dependency parse tree."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"A Winograd schema is a pair of sentences differing in a single word, containing an ambiguous pronoun whose referent differs between the two sentences and requires commonsense or world knowledge to disambiguate.","source_span":"A Winograd schema is a pair of sentences that differ in a single word and that contain an ambiguous pronoun whose referent is different in the two sentences and requires the use of commonsense knowledge or world knowledge to disambiguate."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"The Winograd Schema Challenge (WSC) is administered by commonsensereasoning.org and sponsored by Nuance Inc.","source_span":"The challenge is administered by commonsensereasoning.org and sponsored by Nuance Inc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"The Winograd Schema Challenge was offered for the first time at IJCAI-2016.","source_span":"It was offered for the first time at IJCAI-2016"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"In French, the masculine third-person plural pronoun is 'ils' and the feminine is 'elles'.","source_span":"in French, the masculine pronoun is `ils'; the feminine pronoun is `elles'"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"Of the 144 Winograd schemas in the online collection, 33 can plausibly be converted into a gendered 'they' translation challenge.","source_span":"Of the 144 schemas in the collection at http://www.cs.nyu.edu/faculty/davise/papers/WinogradSchemas/WSCollection.html there are 33 that can plausibly be translated this way."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"Eleven Winograd schemas (numbers 112, 117, 118, 119, 124, 125, 127, 129, 130, 131, and 143) can be converted into a possessive-pronoun (his/her) French-English translation challenge.","source_span":"The eleven Winograd schemas #'s 112, 117, 118, 119, 124, 125, 127, 129, 130, 131, and 143 can be converted this way."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"Winograd schemas are named after Terry Winograd because of a well-known example in his 1970 doctoral thesis.","source_span":"Winograd schemas were named after Terry Winograd because of a well-known example in his doctoral thesis (Winograd 1970)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","statement":"Winograd's original schema sentence was: 'The city councilmen refused to give the women a permit for a demonstration because they [feared/advocated] violence.'","source_span":"Winograd's original schema was: \"The city councilmen refused to give the women a permit for a demonstration because they [feared/advocated] violence\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Who administers the Winograd Schema Challenge and who sponsors it?","answer":"It is administered by commonsensereasoning.org and sponsored by Nuance Inc."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","fact_refs":[2],"question":"At which conference was the Winograd Schema Challenge first offered?","answer":"It was first offered at IJCAI-2016."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Out of the 144 Winograd schemas in the online collection, how many can plausibly be turned into a gendered-pronoun translation challenge using 'they'?","answer":"33 of the 144 schemas can plausibly be translated this way."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the French masculine and feminine forms of the third-person plural pronoun discussed in the paper?","answer":"The masculine form is 'ils' and the feminine form is 'elles'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which eleven Winograd schemas can be converted into a possessive-pronoun (his/her) translation challenge?","answer":"Schemas numbered 112, 117, 118, 119, 124, 125, 127, 129, 130, 131, and 143."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0065","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"Who are Winograd schemas named after, and what was the original example sentence from his thesis?","answer":"They are named after Terry Winograd, from his 1970 doctoral thesis; the original example was 'The city councilmen refused to give the women a permit for a demonstration because they [feared/advocated] violence.'"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"The study proposes a statistical learning approach to predict political bias from text, using standard text features extracted from speeches and manifestos of political parties.","source_span":"This study proposes a simple statistical learning approach to predict political bias from text. Standard text features extracted from speeches and manifestos of political parties are used to predict political bias"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"Political power appears to be strongly correlated with positive sentiment of a text.","source_span":"Political power appears to be strongly correlated with positive sentiment of a text."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"Two approaches are used to make the model's results more interpretable: relating discriminative text features to a party's political orientation, and correlating sentiment features of texts with a measure of political power.","source_span":"Two approaches are presented to make the results more interpretable: a) discriminative text features are related to the political orientation of a party and b) sentiment features of texts are correlated with a measure of political power."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"A web application is presented showing how the model can be used for texts whose political bias is unclear, such as news articles.","source_span":"a web application shows how the model can be used for texts for which the political bias is not clear such as news articles"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"A similar prior approach (BIBREF0) extracted bag-of-words feature vectors and applied linear classifiers to predict political party affiliation of US congress speeches, using data from the House and Senate.","source_span":"The authors extracted BOW feature vectors and applied linear classifiers to predict political party affiliation of US congress speeches. They used data from the two chambers of the US congress, House and Senat, in order to assess generalization performance"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"Two popular dedicated methods for predicting political bias mentioned in related work are WordFish and WordScores.","source_span":"Two popular methods are WordFish BIBREF4 and WordScores BIBREF5"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"Hirst et al.'s experiments on Canadian and European parliament data show that a linear classifier trained on parliament speeches uses language of defense and attack, rather than ideological vocabulary, to classify speeches.","source_span":"The authors show that a linear classifier trained on parliament speeches uses language elements of defense and attack to classify speeches, rather than ideological vocabulary."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","statement":"Hirst et al.'s results show a clear dependency between the length of a political text and the accuracy with which it can be classified correctly.","source_span":"their results show a clear dependency between length of a political text and the accuracy with which it can be classified correctly"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What text sources does this study use to predict political bias, and what method does it use?","answer":"It uses a simple statistical learning approach applied to standard text features extracted from speeches and manifestos of political parties."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is found to be strongly correlated with political power in the study?","answer":"Positive sentiment of a text is found to be strongly correlated with political power."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What two approaches does the study use to make its political bias predictions more interpretable?","answer":"Relating discriminative text features to a party's political orientation, and correlating sentiment features of texts with a measure of political power."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What example application is demonstrated through the study's web application?","answer":"The web application shows how the model can be applied to texts whose political bias is unclear, such as news articles."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What data and method did the prior study (BIBREF0) use to predict political party affiliation of US congress speeches?","answer":"It used bag-of-words feature vectors with linear classifiers, trained and tested on speech data from the two chambers of the US congress, the House and the Senate."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What two popular dedicated methods for predicting political bias are cited in the related work?","answer":"WordFish and WordScores."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0066","fact_refs":[6],"question":"According to Hirst et al.'s experiments on Canadian and European parliament data, what kind of language do classifiers actually rely on to distinguish political speeches?","answer":"They rely on language elements of defense and attack, rather than ideological vocabulary."}
